# The words of a package's answer that the list judges by their WHOLE spelling,
# and what becomes of each (design.md §4.19; panel 198). `libraries.filter_words`
# walks the answer and asks here before it asks about prefixes. Its own module
# because `cli/libraries.hero` stands at its ceiling, as `cli/package_answer.hero`
# is.
#
# The property the list asks of a word is `libraries.hero`'s: does it name a file,
# load anything, or write anything at build time? A word is admitted here by name,
# never by a shape, because no test of a shape is complete (`-Wl,@file`,
# `-Wl,--plugin`, `-Wl,-Map=<file>` each pass a test that asks whether a word
# names a path), and every spelling is the whole word, so a comma that tunnels a
# second option into it (`-Wl,--enable-new-dtags,--plugin,x.so`) is a different
# word and is refused.
#
# **A word PASSES** when clang gives it the one meaning on every platform
# measured and it reaches a compile and a link as it is: `-pthread`, a driver
# spelling of `-D_REENTRANT` and `-lpthread`, which the list already admits.
#
# **A word is LEFT OUT** when it restates what the compiler's own link line
# already says, so passing it adds nothing and one linker refuses it: Apple's
# `ld` stops at `--enable-new-dtags` and at `--export-dynamic` (*ld: unknown
# options*), and `lld-link` warns. Debian's GNU ld 2.44 and LLD 22.1.8 and
# Ubuntu's GNU ld 2.42 write the same bytes with and without them:
# `--enable-new-dtags` is the default tag those toolchains write beside the
# admitted `-Wl,-rpath` (a GNU ld built with upstream's default writes RPATH
# there, the tag a package that says only `-Wl,-rpath` already gets on it), and
# `--export-dynamic` is what clang hands the linker for the `-rdynamic` every
# POSIX link carries (`link_flags.link_flags`). The word that MOVES a tag,
# `--disable-new-dtags`, is not here. Both premises are asked where they can be:
# `tests/harness/absence.hero`'s *an admitted -Wl,-rpath leaves a RUNPATH* and
# the test below on `-rdynamic`.
#
# **`-isystem <dir>` is handed on as `-I<dir>`** (`spelled`), its directory joined
# or as the next word, as clang reads it (Apple clang 21: `-isystem/x` is
# `-isystem` and `/x`). clang quiets every diagnostic LOCATED IN a header it finds
# by `-isystem`, or in a directory it holds for a system one whatever `-I` says,
# and only those: measured on Apple clang 21 and Debian clang 22.1.8, the
# program's own lines, a header macro expanded in them among them, keep every
# `-Werror` the compiler sets, and the header's own inline code loses them.
# **Until panel 205 this premise read that no warning is turned off for a
# header and no bound header is in a system directory**, and both were false:
# `gmp.h` under Linux's `/usr/include` was quiet and built, the same header
# under Homebrew's `-I` was refused at its own line 1882, and on this Mac
# `CPATH` refused it where `C_INCLUDE_PATH` built it (defect 569). Since then
# a header's own code is judged by one region wherever it was found
# (`emit/header_region.hero`: `-Wall`'s warnings and sign conversions passed
# over, a missing return, an uninitialised read, a value narrowed to 32 bits
# and a pointer of another type refused), and every check of a binding is
# raised again after it (`emit/checks_raised.hero`). So the word is still read
# as the directory it names: as `-isystem` it would quiet what the region
# keeps, and a header's verdict would again depend on how a package spells its
# directory. Seven headers under Debian 13's `/usr/include/mit-krb5`, the
# one directory its nine `-isystem` packages name, compile under `-I` with the
# compiler's flags (krb5.h, gssapi/gssapi.h, gssapi/gssapi_krb5.h, gssrpc/rpc.h,
# kadm5/admin.h, kdb.h, profile.h; panel 198's compiler-engineer);
# gssapi/gssapi_alloc.h does not (implicit malloc and free), and none of the six
# entry headers a binding names includes it (panel 198's critic).
#
# **`-Wl,-rpath,<dir>` stays where `eac3e5b5` put it**, and the argument it was
# admitted on stands beside the list again, as that commit (2026-08-14, no
# sitting) wrote it beside `ALLOWED` in the Rust and the port dropped it: an rpath
# names no file, loads nothing and writes nothing at build time and changes what
# the loader picks at run time; a hostile `.pc` saying `-L/tmp/x -lfoo` already
# chooses the library, so it is no new capability, but it lets the choice be
# swapped AFTER the build by anyone who can write that directory. The answer (c)
# of 2026-08-15 left that class to the corpus timeout and refused the list a
# second half: *a guard is not widened to cover a class it cannot see*. So no word
# is admitted here for what it does at run time. **On Windows the rpath is left
# out** (`rpath_words`): a PE loader has none, and `lld-link` warns on `-rpath`
# and then reads its value as an INPUT FILE, so `-Wl,-rpath,h.o` linked an object
# the list never admitted (panel 198's ffi-pragmatist, clang 23.1.1).
#
# **A VALUE IS A NAME, never a file of more words** (route V, from panel 198's
# completeness critic, second pass; defect 527). clang reads an argument
# beginning with `@` as a response file and puts its words in its place, and so
# do Apple's `ld` and GNU `ld` for theirs. Measured with the compiler of that
# sitting and a stand-in `pkg-config`: `-framework @f`, `-Wl,-framework,@f` and
# `-Wl,-rpath,@f` had clang or the linker read `f`'s words as options on macOS
# and Debian 13, a map written and a linker the file named run, every one an
# admitted word. So a value is judged as a value in every slot, joined or as the
# next word (`is_value`): not empty, not a flag (`-`), not a file of words (`@`),
# and for `-l` not GNU ld's `:<file>`, which names a file where `-l` names a
# library (`libuv-static`'s `-l:libuv.a`, defect 528, which ld64 refused at exit
# 2). An `@` inside a path (`openssl@4`) is a letter of the path. The refusal says
# which of these it read (`told`). Its price, refused by construction: an ld64
# rpath macro leading a value, `-Wl,-rpath,@loader_path/../lib`, which no package
# of either census answers.
#
# **`-pthread` reaches a COMPILE everywhere and a LINK everywhere but Windows**,
# where clang 23.1.1 for `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` calls it at a link *argument
# unused during compilation* (measured 2026-10-08): a warning on a correct
# program. The question is the one `link_flags.link_flags` asks, `exe_suffix()`.
#
# **The list is five tables** (panel 198's R3): `VALUED`, `SEPARATE`, `PASSED`,
# `RESTATED` and `LINKED`, which `libraries.filter_words` reads and nothing else
# admits a word. The refusal's note, `libraries.refused`, names them word for
# word, and `libraries.hero`'s test *the refusal's note names the list word for
# word* holds the two together both ways: every word the note names is accepted,
# and every word of a table is named. What is not here stays refused.

use cli/process
use cli/link_flags
use cli/strings

# The flag clang reads with its value as the next word alone (clang's
# `Separate`): `-framework <name>`.
constant SEPARATE: [str]
    [
        "-framework"
    ]

# The words passed on as they are.
constant PASSED: [str]
    [
        "-pthread"
    ]

# The words that say nothing the compiler's own link line does not already say,
# and are left out.
constant RESTATED: [str]
    [
        "-Wl,--enable-new-dtags"
        "-Wl,--export-dynamic"
    ]

# The linker options a `-Wl,` word may carry with one value, `-Wl,<option>,<value>`
# or split as `-Wl,<option> -Wl,<value>`, each checked whole (`linked`).
constant LINKED: [str]
    [
        "-rpath"
        "-framework"
    ]

# Whether `word` is one of `table`, by its whole spelling.
function in_table(word: str, table: [str]) -> bool
    for one in table
        if word == one
            return true

    return false

# Whether `word` is one of `SEPARATE`, its value in the next word.
function separate(word: str) -> bool
    return in_table(word: word, table: SEPARATE)

# Whether `word` is passed on as it is.
function passes(word: str) -> bool
    return in_table(word: word, table: PASSED)

# The flag a package's valued word is handed to clang as: `-isystem` is `-I`.
function spelled(word: str) -> str
    if word == "-isystem"
        return "-I"
    return word

# The flags clang reads with their value joined or as the next word, every one
# in both spellings (defect 160); `-isystem`'s value is handed on as `-I`'s
# (`spelled`). Moved here from `libraries.valued` and `allowed_prefixed`, with
# `-isystem`, when panel 198 took `cli/libraries.hero` past its ceiling.
constant VALUED: [str]
    [
        "-D"
        "-U"
        "-I"
        "-L"
        "-l"
        "-F"
        "-isystem"
    ]

# Whether `word` is one of `VALUED` alone, its value in the next word.
function valued(word: str) -> bool
    return in_table(word: word, table: VALUED)

# The one word a package's `word` is handed on as where the list admits it in one
# word: `-pthread` as it is, and a `VALUED` flag with its value joined, that value
# a value (`is_value`), `-isystem`'s as `-I`'s; an error where the list does not.
function one_word(word: str) -> str?
    if passes(word)
        return ok(word)

    for flag in VALUED
        if strings.starts_with(text: word, prefix: flag) && is_value(flag: flag, value: after(text: word, from: flag.len()))
            return ok(spelled(flag) + after(text: word, from: flag.len()))

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "not a word the list admits whole")

# Whether `value`, written after `flag` or as the value of a `-Wl,` option, is a
# value: not empty, not a flag, not a response file, and for `-l` not `:<file>`.
function is_value(flag: str, value: str) -> bool
    if value.len() == 0
        return false
    first = value.chars()[0]

    if first == "-" || first == "@"
        return false
    return !(flag == "-l" && first == ":")

# What a refusal of `value` owes its reader after the words it names, or "".
function told(flag: str, value: str) -> str
    if value.len() == 0
        return ""
    first = value.chars()[0]

    if first == "@"
        return ": a value beginning with `@` names a file whose words clang and the linker read in its place"

    if flag == "-l" && first == ":"
        return ": `-l:<file>` names a file, where `-l` names a library"
    return ""

# The same, for a word refused whole: a joined value, or a `-Wl,` option's.
function told_word(word: str, next: str) -> str
    for flag in VALUED
        if strings.starts_with(text: word, prefix: flag) && word.len() > flag.len()
            return told(flag: flag, value: after(text: word, from: flag.len()))

    for option in LINKED
        if strings.starts_with(text: word, prefix: "-Wl," + option + ",")
            return told(flag: "", value: after(text: word, from: option.len() + 5))

    if split_option(word: word, next: next)
        return told(flag: "", value: after(text: next, from: 4))
    return ""

# The words a refusal names at `word`: a `-Wl,` option split from its value names
# both.
function named(word: str, next: str) -> str
    if split_option(word: word, next: next)
        return word + " " + next
    return word

# Whether `word` is a `LINKED` option split from its value, `-Wl,<option> -Wl,<value>`.
function split_option(word: str, next: str) -> bool
    for option in LINKED
        if word == "-Wl," + option && strings.starts_with(text: next, prefix: "-Wl,")
            return true

    return false

function after(text: str, from: i64) -> str
    cs = text.chars()
    return cs.slice(from: from, to: cs.len()).join("")

# Whether a LINK takes `word` as it is: `-pthread`, where clang does not call it unused.
function link_passes(word: str) -> bool
    return passes(word) && process.exe_suffix() != ".exe"

# Whether `word` says nothing the compiler's link line does not already say.
function restates(word: str) -> bool
    return in_table(word: word, table: RESTATED)

# The value of a `-Wl,<option>,<value>` word, or of `-Wl,<option>` followed by
# `-Wl,<value>`, for an option of `LINKED`, and how many words it took. Moved
# here from `libraries.linker_value` with panel 198's R3, so the options are a
# table the note is held to.
record Linked
    option: str
    value: str
    words: i64

function linked(word: str, next: str) -> Linked?
    for option in LINKED
        whole = "-Wl," + option + ","

        if strings.starts_with(text: word, prefix: whole) && clean_tail(after(text: word, from: whole.len()))
            return ok(Linked(option: option, value: after(text: word, from: whole.len()), words: 1))

        if word == "-Wl," + option && strings.starts_with(text: next, prefix: "-Wl,") && clean_tail(after(text: next, from: 4))
            return ok(Linked(option: option, value: after(text: next, from: 4), words: 2))

    return fail(code: "none", msg: "not a linker option the list admits")

# The tail of a whole-checked `-Wl,` form: no further comma, and a value
# (`is_value`: not empty, not a flag, not a response file).
function clean_tail(rest: str) -> bool
    for ch in rest.chars()
        if ch == ","
            return false

    return is_value(flag: "-Wl,", value: rest)

# What an admitted linker option becomes on clang's line: an rpath its one word,
# or none on Windows (`rpath_words`); a framework the driver's own pair.
function linked_words(link: Linked) -> [str]
    if link.option == "-framework"
        return ["-framework", link.value]
    return rpath_words(link.value)

# What an admitted rpath becomes: the one word, or none where the linker would
# read its value as a file.
function rpath_words(value: str) -> [str]
    if process.exe_suffix() == ".exe"
        none: [str] = []
        return none
    return ["-Wl,-rpath," + value]

test "a word is passed or left out by its whole spelling, and nothing near it is"
    assert passes("-pthread")
    assert !passes("-pthreads")
    assert !passes("-pthread=1")
    assert !passes("-Wl,-pthread")
    assert !passes("")
    assert link_passes("-pthread") == (process.exe_suffix() != ".exe")
    assert !link_passes("-pthreads")
    assert restates("-Wl,--enable-new-dtags")
    assert restates("-Wl,--export-dynamic")
    assert spelled("-isystem") == "-I"
    assert spelled("-I") == "-I"
    assert spelled("-isystem-after") == "-isystem-after"
    assert one_word("-isystem/sys/a").must() == "-I/sys/a"
    assert one_word("-I/inc").must() == "-I/inc"
    assert one_word("-lz").must() == "-lz"
    assert one_word("-pthread").must() == "-pthread"
    assert one_word("-isystem").is_err()
    assert one_word("-iquote/sys/a").is_err()
    assert one_word("-pthreads").is_err()
    assert one_word("-isystem-after").is_err()
    assert one_word("-I-fplugin=x.so").is_err()
    assert one_word("-I@r").is_err()
    assert one_word("-l:libuv.a").is_err()
    assert valued("-isystem")
    assert !valued("-isystem/sys/a")
    assert !valued("-iquote")

    # The word that moves a tag, a comma that carries a second option, and the
    # position-dependent words are not restatements of anything.
    assert !restates("-Wl,--disable-new-dtags")
    assert !restates("-Wl,--enable-new-dtags,--plugin,x.so")
    assert !restates("-Wl,--export-dynamic,-e,evil")
    assert !restates("--enable-new-dtags")
    assert !restates("-Wl,--no-as-needed")
    assert !restates("-Wl,--whole-archive")
    assert !restates("-Xlinker")

test "a value is a name: not empty, not a flag, not a file of words, and -l not :<file>"
    assert is_value(flag: "-I", value: "/opt/homebrew/Cellar/openssl@4/4.0.3/include")
    assert is_value(flag: "-l", value: "ssl")
    assert !is_value(flag: "-I", value: "")
    assert !is_value(flag: "-I", value: "-fplugin=x.so")
    assert !is_value(flag: "-framework", value: "@rsp")
    assert !is_value(flag: "-Wl,", value: "@loader_path/../lib")
    assert !is_value(flag: "-l", value: ":libuv.a")
    assert is_value(flag: "-L", value: ":odd")
    assert told(flag: "-I", value: "/x") == ""
    assert told_word(word: "-I@r", next: "") == told(flag: "-I", value: "@r")
    assert told_word(word: "-isystem@r", next: "") == told(flag: "-isystem", value: "@r")
    assert told_word(word: "-l:libuv.a", next: "-lm") == ": `-l:<file>` names a file, where `-l` names a library"
    assert told_word(word: "-Wl,-rpath", next: "-Wl,@r") == told(flag: "", value: "@r")
    assert told_word(word: "-Wl,-framework,@r", next: "") != ""
    assert named(word: "-Wl,-rpath", next: "-Wl,@r") == "-Wl,-rpath -Wl,@r"
    assert named(word: "-Wl,-rpath", next: "-lz") == "-Wl,-rpath"

# Every option of `LINKED` is read whole or split and handed on as its own word,
# so a new option without its own words fails here rather than reaching the line
# as another's.
test "every linker option the list admits is read in both spellings and handed on as itself"
    for option in LINKED
        one = linked(word: "-Wl," + option + ",v", next: "").must()
        two = linked(word: "-Wl," + option, next: "-Wl,v").must()
        assert one.option == option && one.value == "v" && one.words == 1
        assert two.option == option && two.value == "v" && two.words == 2
        handed = linked_words(one)

        if option == "-framework"
            assert handed == ["-framework", "v"]

        if option != "-framework"
            assert option == "-rpath"
            assert handed == rpath_words("v")

    assert linked(word: "-Wl,-rpath,a,b", next: "").is_err()
    assert linked(word: "-Wl,-rpath", next: "-Wl,-e,evil").is_err()
    assert linked(word: "-Wl,-rpath", next: "-L/lib").is_err()
    assert linked(word: "-Wl,-rpath,@r", next: "").is_err()
    assert linked(word: "-Wl,-e,main", next: "").is_err()
    assert separate("-framework")
    assert !separate("-frameworkCocoa")

# Where the rpath means something it is one word, and on Windows none.
test "an admitted rpath is one word where a loader reads it, and none on Windows"
    if process.exe_suffix() == ".exe"
        assert rpath_words("/opt/lib").len() == 0

    if process.exe_suffix() != ".exe"
        assert rpath_words("/opt/lib") == ["-Wl,-rpath,/opt/lib"]

# The premise of the second word `restates` holds, asked of the line itself: the
# compiler's own POSIX link already carries `-rdynamic`, which clang hands the
# linker as `-export-dynamic`, so a package's `-Wl,--export-dynamic` adds nothing
# (Debian GNU ld 2.44 and LLD 22.1.8: the same bytes with and without it). The day
# `link_flags.link_flags` stops saying it, the word restates nothing and this fires.
test "the link line carries -rdynamic on every POSIX link, which --export-dynamic restates"
    if process.exe_suffix() != ".exe"
        said: bool @= false

        for flag in link_flags.link_flags()
            if flag == "-rdynamic"
                said @ true

        assert said
